// Practice quiz: Polymorphism: overriding vs overloading
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/polymorphism-overriding-vs-overloading

::o1 runtime choice::Animal a holds a new Dog(), and Dog overrides speak(). Which speak() runs for a.speak()? {
~Animal's speak(), because a is declared Animal#Not quite. The declared type decides what may be called; the object decides which override runs.
~Both, Animal's first#Not quite. Only one version runs unless Dog's method calls super.speak().
=Dog's speak()#Correct. Overridden instance methods are chosen from the object's runtime class, not the variable's declared type.
~It does not compile#Not quite. Calling a method that Animal declares through an Animal reference is legal.
}

::o1 the annotation::What does @Override on a method do? {
~Makes the method override even when the signature differs#Not quite. The signature must still match; the annotation only checks.
~Changes which version runs at run time#Not quite. Dispatch is the same with or without the annotation.
~Prevents subclasses from overriding the method#Not quite. That is what final does.
=Asks the compiler to report an error if the method does not override or implement a supertype method#Correct. It catches mistakes such as a misspelled name or a different parameter list.
}

::o2 same name new parameters::A subclass adds void print(String s), and its superclass has void print(int n). What is the relationship? {
~Overriding, because the names match#Not quite. The parameter types differ, so it is a new method.
~A compile error for a duplicate method#Not quite. Different parameter lists make the methods distinct.
~Hiding the superclass method#Not quite. print(int n) is still inherited and callable.
=Overloading: a second method with different parameters#Correct. Overriding requires the same name and the same parameter types.
}

::o2 when chosen::When is an overloaded method chosen, and when is an overridden method chosen? {
~Both are chosen at run time#Not quite. Overload selection uses the static argument types, at compile time.
=Overloads are chosen at compile time from argument types; overrides at run time from the object#Correct. The compiler picks the signature, and the runtime object picks the override of that signature.
~Both are chosen at compile time#Not quite. Override selection happens when the call runs.
~Overloads at run time; overrides at compile time#Not quite. That reverses the two rules.
}

::o3 superclass array::An array of Shape holds a Circle and a Square, each overriding area(). A loop calls s.area() for each element. What happens? {
=Each element runs its own class's area()#Correct. One call through a Shape reference reaches each object's override.
~Every element runs Shape's area()#Not quite. The declared element type does not choose the override.
~It does not compile without casts#Not quite. area() is declared on Shape, so no cast is needed.
~Only the first element's version runs for all#Not quite. Dispatch happens separately for every call.
}

::o3 calling subclass-only methods::Shape s holds a Circle. Circle adds radius(), which Shape does not declare. Can you call s.radius()? {
=No; the compiler only allows methods declared on Shape#Correct. Through a Shape reference you can call Shape's methods; you would need a Circle reference for radius().
~Yes, because the object is a Circle#Not quite. The declared type limits what the compiler lets you call.
~Yes, but it returns 0#Not quite. The call does not compile at all.
~Yes, after adding @Override#Not quite. radius() overrides nothing, so the annotation would be an error.
}
